WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:05.440
Hey there and thanks for stopping by. My name is&nbsp;
Bradley Knapp and I'm one of the Product Managers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:10.560
here at IBM Cloud, and the question that we're&nbsp;
going to answer today is what is the difference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:15.680
between DevOps and SRE? This is a question&nbsp;
that I hear on a fairly regular basis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:20.400
not just internally, but from external customers&nbsp;
as well. And it's one that we'd like to help you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:24.880
walk through so that you can really figure out&nbsp;
what makes sense in your organization and I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:29.600
the answer is probably going to surprise you a&nbsp;
little bit. Before we get into the video that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:34.080
do want to encourage you to like and subscribe, if&nbsp;
you think that you're going to enjoy these things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:37.520
just click on those buttons that way you&nbsp;
get notified every time we come out with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:43.840
something new. So, with that let's get right&nbsp;
into the question and the question is DevOps

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:49.840
versus SRE.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:57.840
And so, as we get into this, I think probably&nbsp;
the most important thing to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:05.440
is this isn't a versus question. You don't have&nbsp;
to have one or the other. As a matter of fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:11.760
I would argue and I think that many people would&nbsp;
agree that SRE is actually an essential component&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:20.000
of DevOps. And DevOps, a good properly implemented&nbsp;
DevOps method, leads to the necessity of SRE when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:25.360
it comes time to deploy. There are two sides of&nbsp;
the same coin. And so, that's obviously going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:30.240
lead to a little bit of confusion because DevOps&nbsp;
is the development methodology, right. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:34.400
it's all about integrating your development&nbsp;
teams and your operations teams. It's about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:40.320
knocking down those silos between them. It's about&nbsp;
ensuring that everybody is singing off the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:47.360
song book and that's very important. And SRE is in&nbsp;
charge of automating all of the things and making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:54.560
sure that you never go down. There are really two&nbsp;
parts of the same group, and so let's look at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.560 --> 00:02:00.080
differences, right, because they do have some&nbsp;
differences. Probably the first and largest one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:06.000
is that when we think about our DevOps site over&nbsp;
here, right, DevOps is about core development.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:12.960
The DevOps guys, particularly your developers,&nbsp;
they are doing the core development,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:18.400
they are answering the question "what do we&nbsp;
want to do?", they are working with product,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:24.240
they're working with sales, they're working with&nbsp;
marketing to develop design and deploy. What is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:29.680
it that we do? They're working on the core. SRE&nbsp;
on the other hand, they're not working on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:35.840
core. What they are working is the implementation&nbsp;
of the core, they are working on the deployment,

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:45.680
and they are constantly giving feedback back&nbsp;
into that core development group to say "hey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:50.880
something that you guys have designed isn't&nbsp;
working exactly the way that you think that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:56.160
is." So, if we were to break that down a little&nbsp;
bit more they are helping the DevOps group,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:02.000
our SRE group is helping the DevOps group to&nbsp;
break down even more of those silos. If you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:08.160
want to think about it this way DevOps is trying&nbsp;
to develop the answer to how do we solve this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:15.680
problem, SRE is saying how do we deploy and&nbsp;
maintain and run to solve this problem it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:21.680
the theoretical versus the practical, and ideally&nbsp;
they're talking to each other every day, right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:27.120
because SRE should be logging defects, they should&nbsp;
be logging tickets back with development, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:32.080
probably most importantly they need to understand&nbsp;
that they have the same goals. These groups should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:38.000
never be aligned against one another. And so,&nbsp;
they do have to have a common understanding.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:41.520
Let's talk about one of the most important&nbsp;
parts, right, we're going to talk about failure

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:50.880
because failure is not necessary failure, it's&nbsp;
just a way of life. It doesn't matter what you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:55.840
deploy. It doesn't matter how well it&nbsp;
goes, it's going to happen. And so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:02.000
when we talk about failure everyone involved needs&nbsp;
to understand that there's going to be some level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:06.160
right. There is a failure budget, or an error&nbsp;
budget, where things are going to go wrong.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:11.920
And what happens when things go wrong that's what&nbsp;
figures out whether or not your organization is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:18.000
working because your SRE team when it comes&nbsp;
to failure, they're going to anticipate it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.280
they're going to monitor it, they're going to&nbsp;
log it, they're going to record everything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:26.720
and ideally they can identify a failure before&nbsp;
it happens. They're going to have predictive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:30.640
analytics that are going to say "all right this&nbsp;
thing is going to go bad based on what we've seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:36.080
before." And so, SRE is responsible for mitigating&nbsp;
some of those failures through monitoring and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:42.000
logging, and doing the preemptive parts, right.&nbsp;
So we'll do the monitors, we'll do the logs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:51.440
SRE is also going to lead all of your post actual&nbsp;
failure incident management, right. They're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:55.200
to get you through the incident to begin with and&nbsp;
then they're going to hot wash it when it's done.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.200 --> 00:05:03.680
They're going to lead that RCA, that root cause&nbsp;
analysis, and after they have that RCA completed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:09.200
and this is the most important part they&nbsp;
have to take that RCA data and bring it back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:16.880
over into dev and get some tickets open. You&nbsp;
have to get dev online because you've gotta,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:21.920
these are the guys who are gonna solve the&nbsp;
core problem, some RCAs might be solved by SRE&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:26.000
internally, right. They're gonna spend 50 percent&nbsp;
of their time writing, 50 of their time working,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:31.760
and so some of that problem they may be able to&nbsp;
fix directly, but sometimes that's not the case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:37.040
right. Our RCA may have found a problem&nbsp;
that only dev can fix and that's all right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:41.840
that's not a big deal. They're going to get&nbsp;
that over here, dev is going to implement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:45.280
and then probably the most important part,&nbsp;
right, so you're going to get that new feature.

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:53.920
Dev is going to get that pulled together.&nbsp;
They're going to get that new feature rolled out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:57.600
and then they're going to pass that back&nbsp;
into SRE and they're going to say "hey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:01.440
SRE, that problem that we had&nbsp;
we got a new feature for you."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:06.720
And then our guys on the SRE side, what do&nbsp;
they do? They then have to take that feature

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:15.760
and they have to figure out how to integrate it&nbsp;
into their monitoring and their logging efforts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:21.520
to make sure that we don't get into&nbsp;
another RCA for the same kind of a problem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:27.600
So these groups, they are part and parcel&nbsp;
of the same bunch. You really can't have one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:32.960
successful organization without another. And&nbsp;
when it comes to figuring out a distinction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:37.280
it's not something that you should spend a lot&nbsp;
of time with. There are different skill sets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:42.880
right. Core development DevOps, these are the guys&nbsp;
that really love writing software. SRE is a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:47.040
bit more of an investigative mindset, right. You&nbsp;
have to be willing to go and do that analysis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:51.200
figure out what things have gone wrong, automate&nbsp;
all of the things. But there's a lot that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:56.240
have in common. Everyone should be writing&nbsp;
automation, everyone should be getting rid of toil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:01.760
as much as possible because we just don't have the&nbsp;
time to be doing manual tasks. When we can put the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:06.880
computers in charge of it, right, computers are&nbsp;
not great at thinking on their own, but if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:12.880
need it to do the same thing over and over and&nbsp;
over again in exactly the same way you can't beat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:18.320
computing for that. And so, automation is key,&nbsp;
you just have a slightly different mindset. DevOps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:22.000
is going to automate deployment, they're going to&nbsp;
automate tasks, they're going to automate feature.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:27.760
SRE is going to automate redundancy, and they're&nbsp;
going to automate manual tasks that they can turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:35.040
into programmatic tasks to keep the stack up. And&nbsp;
so, you know when we talk about DevOps versus SRE,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:40.400
that's not the question, the question is&nbsp;
how do we build DevOps, how do we build SRE,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:45.680
and how do we be sure that they are always talking&nbsp;
to each other because the institutional knowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:51.360
that SRE has so much of if that doesn't get passed&nbsp;
back into your DevOps group. You're never going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:56.240
to be successful, you're going to have a silo&nbsp;
here, and a silo here, and at the end of the day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.240 --> 00:08:03.200
both of these philosophies core, core component,&nbsp;
is getting rid of silos, freeing ourselves from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:09.680
those silos is what is going to make us all more&nbsp;
successful. Thank you so much for stopping by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:13.840
the channel today. If you have any questions or&nbsp;
comments, please feel free to share them with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:18.320
us below. If you enjoyed this video and you&nbsp;
would like to see more like it in the future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:22.640
please do like the video and subscribe to us&nbsp;
so that we'll know to keep creating for you.

